  "worked_solution": "Over the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nLet's calculate the average rate of change of $f(x)$ over the interval $[3,c]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x} & =\\frac{f(c)-f(3)}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{({c}^{2}-2c)-({3}^{2}-2\\cdot 3)}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}^{2}-2c-3}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(c-3)(c+1)}{c-3} \\\\  & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want this average rate of change to be equal to $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u0394f}{\u0394x} & =6 \\\\ c+1 & =6 \\\\ c & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
